ui-icon-buttons-represent-common-actions-with-a-recognizable-icon-use-them-when-the-action-is = Icon buttons represent common actions with a recognizable icon. Use them when the action is easy to understand without a persistent text label.

ui-choose-a-default-button-for-an-action-and-a-toggle-button-for-a-persistent-selection-use-f = • Choose a default button for an action and a toggle button for a persistent selection.
    • Use filled, tonal, outlined or standard colors to establish hierarchy.
    • Choose among five sizes, two shapes and three widths.
    • On the web, show a concise tooltip on hover and keyboard focus.
    • Toggle icons can change from outlined when unselected to filled when selected.
    • Give every action a meaningful accessible name and a target of at least 48 × 48dp.

ui-use-icon-buttons-for-familiar-frequently-used-actions-such-as-favoriting-archiving-or-mark = Use icon buttons for familiar, frequently used actions such as favoriting, archiving or marking a message as read. Keep the most useful commands visible and place secondary actions in an overflow menu. Prefer a text button when the icon alone would be ambiguous.

ui-the-heart-retains-its-selected-state-archive-moves-this-message-out-of-the-inbox-mark-as-r = The heart retains its selected state. Archive moves this message out of the inbox, Mark as read changes its read state, and More options opens a real menu with local message actions.

ui-an-icon-button-contains-an-icon-an-optional-visible-container-and-a-larger-interactive-tar = An icon button contains an icon, an optional visible container and a larger interactive target. Container shape and color communicate emphasis; the accessible name describes the action rather than the picture.

ui-use-filled-icon-buttons-for-strong-emphasis-tonal-buttons-for-a-softer-container-outlined = Use filled icon buttons for strong emphasis, tonal buttons for a softer container, outlined buttons for a visible boundary and standard buttons for the least emphasis. Match colors to the surrounding surface and keep the icon distinguishable in every enabled state.

ui-choose-a-size-that-matches-the-importance-and-context-of-the-action-small-controls-suit-de = Choose a size that matches the importance and context of the action. Small controls suit dense interfaces; larger sizes emphasize key actions or sit alongside larger content. Keep at least a 48 × 48dp interactive target even when the visible extra-small or small container is smaller.

ui-width-changes-the-container-s-horizontal-proportions-without-changing-the-icon-size-or-the = Width changes the container's horizontal proportions without changing the icon size or the button's height. Use narrow buttons where space is limited, default width for balanced proportions and wide buttons when an action needs more horizontal emphasis. Do not shrink the interactive target below 48dp.

ui-narrow = Narrow

ui-wide = Wide

ui-round-and-square-shapes-offer-different-visual-character-pressed-shapes-share-the-same-siz = Round and square shapes offer different visual character. Pressed shapes share the same size-specific corner radius; selection swaps round and square shapes. Keep the shape language consistent within a group of related actions.

ui-default-icon-buttons-perform-an-action-when-activated-toggle-icon-buttons-retain-selection = Default icon buttons perform an action when activated. Toggle icon buttons retain selection and expose that state to assistive technology. Hover and focus feedback help people identify the current target; pressed feedback confirms contact, and activation occurs only for a completed press.

